DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/el-tiempo-no-para-memoria-y-h
 istoria-para-pensar-proyectos-de-futuro-en-el-cono-sur\n\nCo-sponsored by 
 the Consulate General of Uruguay in San Francisco\n\nIn this lecture\, aut
 hors Aldo Marchesi y Vania Markarian will present their book El tiempo no 
 para. The book aims to trace the history of the idea of human rights and t
 o understand the impact of that concept on the development of democracies 
 at the close of the 20th century and in progressive governments of the 21s
 t century. It further puts forth questions on the limits of the language o
 f human rights when considering the contemporary crisis. \n\nAldo Marchesi
  holds a PhD in History from New York University and is a Professor in the
  Department of Theory and Historiography at the Institute for History at T
 he University of the Republic\, as well as a member of the Center for Urug
 uayan Interdisciplinary Studies. Together with colleagues from other disci
 plines\, he recently founded the Institute for Research on Social Justice 
 and Inequality. He is a member of the National System of Researchers in Ur
 uguay. He has served as a visiting professor at universities and research 
 centers in the United States\, Chile\, Argentina\, Colombia\, and China. H
 is work has focused on the recent history of Uruguay and the Southern Cone
 . He has published numerous articles and books. His first book\, reissued 
 in 2023\, was El Uruguay Inventado: La política audiovisual durante la di
 ctadura. His most recent book\, a regional history of the armed Left in th
 e Southern Cone\, was published in Spanish and English under the title Hac
